instruction:
Write three test files. Two API unit files and one PostgreSQL-backed integration file.

UNIT FILES run under apps/api/vitest.config.ts: environment node, NO setupFiles, so there is NO database and NO env. The established local idiom (see tests/api/label/delete-label.test.ts and tests/api/utils/workspace-access-middleware.test.ts) is to vi.mock the database module and import the real code under test. Follow it - do NOT reconstruct a copy of the validator schema, which would test a duplicate rather than the shipped one.

In each unit file:
  vi.mock("../../../apps/api/src/database", async () => {
    const schema = await import("../../../apps/api/src/database/schema");
    return { default: <chainable stub>, schema };
  });
Also vi.mock("../../../apps/api/src/utils/workspace-access-middleware") exporting workspaceAccess with fromProject/fromColumn returning a pass-through middleware ((c, next) => next()), and vi.mock("../../../apps/api/src/utils/require-workspace-permission") exporting requireWorkspacePermission as () => (c, next) => next(). Then `import column from "../../../apps/api/src/column/index"` and drive it with column.request(path, { method, headers: { "content-type": "application/json" }, body: JSON.stringify(...) }).
Validator middleware runs BEFORE the access middleware in the chain, so a malformed body returns 400 without any database work.

tests/api/column/create-column.test.ts - POST /:projectId. Assert 400 for wipLimit of 0, -3, 2.5, 2147483648, 99999999999, 9007199254740992, Number.MAX_VALUE (assert each value individually so a regression names the value that broke). Assert NOT-400 for wipLimit null, key absent, 1, 5, 2147483647.
tests/api/column/update-column.test.ts - PUT /:id, the same accept and reject matrix, plus a case proving an explicit null is accepted as a clear.

INTEGRATION FILE tests/api-integration/column-wip-limit.test.ts runs under vitest.integration.config.ts (real PostgreSQL, single worker, setup.ts supplies DATABASE_URL and env). Follow tests/api-integration/label.test.ts exactly: import db, { schema } from "../../apps/api/src/database"; import { createApp } from "../../apps/api/src/index"; import { mockAuthenticatedSession } from "./helpers/auth"; import { resetTestDatabase } from "./helpers/database"; import { createProjectFixture, createWorkspaceMember } from "./helpers/fixtures"; beforeEach(async () => { await resetTestDatabase(); }).
Cover: (a) POST /api/column/:projectId with wipLimit 5 persists 5 and the row reads back 5; (b) omitting wipLimit yields null on the persisted row; (c) PUT /api/column/:id with wipLimit null clears a previously set limit; (d) PUT with wipLimit 0 returns 400 and leaves the stored value unchanged; (e) GET the tasks board endpoint and assert each projected column carries wipLimit, set for the configured column and null for the others; (f) ADVISORY PROOF - with a column whose wipLimit is 1, creating a second task in that column still succeeds (no 4xx), because the limit never blocks.
Note the board projection sets id to the column slug, so match projected columns by slug.

Return STRICT JSON: {"files":[{"path":"...","content":"<entire file>"}]} with all three files.

Add the per-column WIP-limit control to apps/web/src/components/project/column-editor.tsx. This file is the configuration surface; the board header is display-only.

(1) Add this handler next to the existing handleToggleFinal, mirroring its shape exactly:
const handleWipLimitChange = async (id: string, wipLimit: number | null) => {
  try {
    await updateColumn({ id, projectId, data: { wipLimit } });
    toast.success(t("settings:columnEditor.toastWipLimitUpdated"));
  } catch (error) {
    toast.error(
      error instanceof Error
        ? error.message
        : t("settings:columnEditor.toastWipLimitUpdateError"),
    );
  }
};

(2) In the per-column row, inside the <div className="flex items-center gap-1.5 shrink-0"> cluster and BEFORE the Done-column toggle group (the div titled settings:columnEditor.doneColumnTooltip), insert:
<div className="flex items-center gap-2" title={t("settings:columnEditor.wipLimitTooltip")}>
  <span className="text-xs text-muted-foreground whitespace-nowrap">
    {t("settings:columnEditor.wipLimit")}
  </span>
  <Input
    type="number"
    inputMode="numeric"
    min={1}
    max={2147483647}
    step={1}
    defaultValue={col.wipLimit ?? ""}
    placeholder={t("settings:columnEditor.wipLimitPlaceholder")}
    disabled={!canEdit}
    aria-label={t("settings:columnEditor.wipLimitAria", { name: col.name })}
    className="h-7 w-16 text-xs"
    onBlur={(e) => {
      const raw = e.target.value.trim();
      const next = raw === "" ? null : Number(raw);
      const current = col.wipLimit ?? null;
      if (next === current) return;
      if (
        next !== null &&
        (!Number.isInteger(next) || next < 1 || next > 2147483647)
      ) {
        e.target.value = current === null ? "" : String(current);
        return;
      }
      handleWipLimitChange(col.id, next);
    }}
    onKeyDown={(e) => {
      if (e.key === "Enter") {
        e.preventDefault();
        e.currentTarget.blur();
      }
    }}
  />
</div>

Input, t, toast, updateColumn and canEdit all already exist in this file - add no imports. col.id here is the real column id from useGetColumns (a full row select), which is why configuration lives here rather than in the header. min/max/step mirror the API validator as a UX guard only; the API stays authoritative. Change nothing else.

Return STRICT JSON: {"edits":[{"path","old_string","new_string","reason"}]}.

Replace the plain task-count badge in apps/web/src/components/kanban-board/column/column-header.tsx with a count/limit badge carrying an advisory over-cap indicator. DISPLAY ONLY - this file must not gain any input, mutation, or blocking behavior.

REQUIRED IMPORTS - this file today imports only { Archive, Plus } from lucide-react and has no cn import. Both must be added or the file fails typecheck on two missing symbols:
  - extend the lucide-react import to: import { AlertTriangle, Archive, Plus } from "lucide-react";
  - add: import { cn } from "@/lib/utils";
Keep the repo's existing import ordering convention (the @/ imports are grouped together and alphabetized by path).

Inside the component, before the returned JSX, compute:
  const count = column.tasks.length;
  const limit = column.wipLimit ?? null;
  const over = limit !== null && count > limit;

Replace this existing element:
        <span className="rounded-md bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-muted-foreground">
          {column.tasks.length}
        </span>
with:
        <span
          className={cn(
            "inline-flex items-center gap-1 rounded-md bg-muted px-1.5 py-0.5 text-xs font-medium text-muted-foreground",
            over && "bg-destructive/15 text-destructive",
          )}
          aria-label={
            limit === null
              ? t("tasks:kanban.taskCountAria", { count })
              : t("tasks:kanban.wipLimitAria", { count, limit })
          }
          title={
            limit === null
              ? undefined
              : over
                ? t("tasks:kanban.wipLimitOver", { count, limit })
                : t("tasks:kanban.wipLimitWithin", { count, limit })
          }
        >
          {limit === null ? count : `${count} / ${limit}`}
          {over && <AlertTriangle className="w-3 h-3" aria-hidden="true" />}
        </span>

Over-cap is strictly count > limit - a column sitting exactly at its limit renders neutral, and there is NO at-capacity state. The indicator must not be color-only: the AlertTriangle icon plus the title and aria-label are the non-color affordance and are required. Leave the archive button, add-task button and both modals untouched. Do not touch column-dropzone.tsx or kanban-board/column/index.tsx.

Return STRICT JSON: {"edits":[{"path","old_string","new_string","reason"}]}.

Write two component test files following the established local idiom in apps/web/src/components/list-view/task-row.test.tsx: every hook and store dependency is vi.mock'd at module level, and react-i18next is mocked so t returns the KEY. Assertions therefore match key strings such as "tasks:kanban.wipLimitOver", never English text.

Shared boilerplate:
import { cleanup, fireEvent, render, screen } from "@testing-library/react";
import { afterEach, describe, expect, it, vi } from "vitest";
afterEach(() => { cleanup(); vi.clearAllMocks(); });
vi.mock("react-i18next", () => ({
  useTranslation: () => ({ t: (key: string) => key }),
  initReactI18next: { type: "3rdParty", init: vi.fn() },
}));

FILE 1 apps/web/src/components/kanban-board/column/column-header.test.tsx - imports { ColumnHeader } from "./column-header" (a NAMED export). Mock exactly these, matching the specifiers the component uses:
  "@/store/project" -> { default: () => ({ project: { id: "project-1" }, setProject: vi.fn() }) }
  "@/hooks/mutations/task/use-update-task" -> { useUpdateTask: () => ({ mutate: vi.fn() }) }
  "@/hooks/use-workspace-permission" -> { useWorkspacePermission: () => ({ canUpdateTasks: () => true, canCreateTasks: () => true }) }
  "@/lib/column" -> { getColumnIcon: () => null }
  "@/lib/toast" -> { toast: { success: vi.fn(), error: vi.fn() } }
  "@/components/shared/modals/create-task-modal" -> { default: () => null }
  "../../shared/modals/archive-tasks-modal" -> { ArchiveTasksModal: () => null }
Build a column fixture helper taking (count, wipLimit) and returning { id: "to-do", slug: "to-do", name: "To do", icon: null, isFinal: false, wipLimit, tasks: Array.from({length: count}, (_, i) => ({ id: `t${i}` })) } cast through `as unknown as ...` to satisfy the prop type without hand-writing a full Task.
Cases: (a) wipLimit null -> badge text is exactly the count, no slash, and aria-label is "tasks:kanban.taskCountAria"; (b) count 3 limit 5 -> text "3 / 5", title "tasks:kanban.wipLimitWithin", NO svg icon inside the badge; (c) count 6 limit 5 -> text "6 / 5", title "tasks:kanban.wipLimitOver", and the badge contains an svg; (d) BOUNDARY: count 5 limit 5 -> title is "tasks:kanban.wipLimitWithin" and no svg, proving equal-to-limit is NOT over-cap.

FILE 2 apps/web/src/components/project/column-editor.test.tsx - imports ColumnEditor from "./column-editor" (a DEFAULT export), rendered as <ColumnEditor projectId="project-1" />. Hoist a shared const mockUpdateColumn = vi.fn() via vi.hoisted so the assertions can see it. Mock:
  "@/hooks/queries/column/use-get-columns" -> { useGetColumns: () => ({ data: [{ id: "col-1", slug: "to-do", name: "To do", icon: null, isFinal: false, wipLimit: null, position: 0 }], isLoading: false }) }
  "@/hooks/mutations/column/use-update-column" -> { useUpdateColumn: () => ({ mutateAsync: mockUpdateColumn }) }
  "@/hooks/mutations/column/use-create-column", "...use-delete-column", "...use-reorder-columns" -> each returning { mutateAsync: vi.fn() } under its own named export
  "@/hooks/use-workspace-permission" -> { useWorkspacePermission: () => ({ canManageProjects: () => true }) }
  "@/lib/column" -> { getColumnIcon: () => null }
  "@/lib/toast" -> { toast: { success: vi.fn(), error: vi.fn() } }
  "@/constants/column-icons" -> { default: {}, DEFAULT_COLUMN_ICON_NAMES: {} }
Find the input by its aria-label key "settings:columnEditor.wipLimitAria" (t returns the key, so the label is that literal string).
Cases: (a) change value to "5" then blur -> mockUpdateColumn called once with { id: "col-1", projectId: "project-1", data: { wipLimit: 5 } }; (b) with a column whose wipLimit is 5, clearing to "" then blur -> called with data: { wipLimit: null }; (c) value "0" then blur -> NOT called, and the input reverts; (d) value "2147483648" then blur -> NOT called; (e) unchanged value then blur -> NOT called. Use a second describe block or a re-mock for the case needing an initial wipLimit of 5.

